<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Time Capsule</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Am Preparing for the Next Five Years</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts. Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze similarities and differences in articles on skill development (e.g., "Both articles mention communication and critical thinking."). Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in two articles about future careers, with examples related to Panama (e.g., "The first article states that coding jobs are growing globally, while the second article argues that Panama needs to increase training for tech roles."). Can compare views in two texts about career paths for students (e.g., "I think this article about talking to family members about how they learned their skills is better advice than just reading about jobs on the internet.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various high-demand careers in Panama (e.g., "software developer," "data analyst," "engineering," "medicine"). Students are asked to quickly read the job titles and discuss in pairs which careers they find most "future-oriented" and why, using vocabulary such as "possibilities," "opportunities," and "high-demand." The teacher ensures students recall vocabulary from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for keywords and main ideas by demonstrating with a short paragraph on the board. For example, "I'll quickly read this paragraph about 'critical thinking' to understand its core message." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of words containing the long /iː/ sound (e.g., "achieve," "believe," "engineering," "leadership") and stress in multisyllabic words like "problem-solving" and "community service."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'high-demand career' mean? Does it mean few people want it or many people need it?" (Expected: Many people need it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a text is 'future-oriented,' what kind of information do you expect to find?" (Expected: Information about what will happen in the future, predictions, plans.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive two short informational articles (e.g., blog posts, news excerpts) about future career trends and necessary skills in Panama. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">*   **Text 1: "Panama's Tech Horizon: Growing Opportunities in Software Development and Data Analysis"** (Factual, discussing market growth, need for "training," "skills," "technology," "internship" experience, "high-demand" roles). It includes statements like, "The Panamanian government *will be investing* heavily in digital infrastructure over the next five years."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">*   **Text 2: "Beyond the Screen: Why Soft Skills Are Critical for Future Success in Panama"** (More opinion-based, emphasizing "leadership," "problem-solving," "critical thinking," "community service," and "adaptable" skills, "advice" from mentors). It might include statements like, "I think students *should* focus more on developing these 'practical' skills rather than just 'studying' for exams." It might also report advice: "Experts *suggested that* students 'volunteer' to 'gain' 'experience'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to read and understand the main ideas and specific details in short informational texts about future plans and career development, and to differentiate between facts and opinions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read both texts individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Gist Question 1:** "What is the main topic of Text 1?" (e.g., "Technology careers in Panama").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Gist Question 2:** "What is the main idea Text 2 wants to convey?" (e.g., "The importance of soft skills").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**True/False:** Students complete 3-4 True/False statements based on factual information in both texts. (e.g., "True or False: Text 1 states that 'coding jobs are growing globally'." or "True or False: Text 2 'recommends' focusing solely on academic subjects.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific details, identifying facts and opinions, using modals for possibilities, future continuous for ongoing plans, and reported speech for sharing advice. Key vocabulary: "career," "future," "goals," "possibilities," "advice," "education," "training," "technology," "skills," "leadership," "problem-solving," "critical thinking," "opportunities," "pursue," "develop," "research," "recommend."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Fact vs. Opinion Identification (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with 5-6 sentences extracted from the two articles. They identify each statement as either a "Fact" or an "Opinion." The teacher guides them, explaining that facts can be verified, while opinions reflect a belief or judgment. E.g., "The first article states that coding jobs are growing globally." (Fact) vs. "The second article argues that Panama needs to increase training for tech roles." (Opinion/Argument).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar Spotting & Rephrasing (Semi-controlled):</b> Students scan both texts again to find examples of:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Modals for career possibilities:** They highlight sentences like "I *might pursue* a career in 'software development'." or "Students *can develop* 'leadership' 'skills' through 'community service'."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Future continuous for ongoing plans:** They find sentences such as "In five years, I *will be working as* a 'data analyst'." or "Panama *will be investing* in 'technology' infrastructure."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Reported speech:** They locate advice given in the articles and rephrase it using reported speech. E.g., if an article says, "You *should* 'research' 'high-demand' 'skills'," students write, "The article *suggested that* students *should research* 'high-demand' 'skills'."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer-Check and Vocabulary Reinforcement (Pair Interaction):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from activity 1 and 2. They discuss any discrepancies and justify their choices by referring back to the texts. They use target vocabulary to explain their reasoning. The teacher circulates, providing assistance and monitoring pronunciation of /iː/ sounds in words like "achieve" and "believe," and stress in "critical thinking."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Time Capsule")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading Analysis and Comparison (Group Discussion and Chart Completion).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In small groups (3-4 students), learners will collaboratively analyze the two articles to prepare for their "Future Plans and Time Capsule" project. Each group receives a Venn diagram or a comparison chart template. They must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify and list **similarities** between the two articles regarding future "career" "goals" or essential "skills" (e.g., "Both articles mention 'communication' and 'critical thinking'.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify and list **differences** in their main arguments or focus (e.g., "One article focuses on 'technology' and 'training,' while the other emphasizes 'leadership' and 'community service'.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Select **two facts** from each article and **two opinions/arguments** from each article, explaining their reasoning for the classification. For opinions, they should try to express a counter-argument or agreement. E.g., "I think this article about talking to family members about how they learned their 'skills' is better 'advice' than just reading about jobs on the internet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss which article offers more relevant "advice" for their personal "future plans" and time capsule item choices, using modals of possibility (e.g., "I *might pursue* a 'career' related to 'engineering' because Text 1 highlights its 'opportunities'.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher circulates, observing group dynamics, listening for the use of target vocabulary, grammar (modals, future continuous, reported speech), and pronunciation. The teacher encourages students to assign roles within their group (e.g., "Let's have a recorder to write down everyone's 'goals' and a presenter to summarize our discussion.") to facilitate sociolinguistic competence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and review of the completed comparison charts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their main findings from the comparison chart, highlighting the most significant similarities and differences, and providing one example of a fact and an opinion from the texts, justifying their classification. They also share one piece of "advice" they found most useful from the articles for their "future."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in identifying similarities and differences between texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in distinguishing between facts and opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary ("career," "skills," "advice," "opportunities," "critical thinking," "problem-solving," "future-oriented").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary and multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to explain choices for their 'future goals' based on the articles (Pragmatic).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about 'future careers' from the readings is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was easy/difficult to distinguish facts from opinions because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has reading these articles influenced my 'future plans' or my thinking about the 'skills' I need to 'develop'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of analyzing texts (finding similarities/differences, facts/opinions) did I find most challenging, and how can I 'improve'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students analyze texts and distinguish facts from opinions? Was the difficulty level of the texts appropriate for B1.1?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which vocabulary items or grammatical structures (modals, future continuous, reported speech) did students struggle with most during the reading and discussion tasks? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I leverage the insights from today's reading analysis to prepare students for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction)? I'll focus on activities that require them to verbally express their own "future goals," "advice" they've received, and use the comparative language from today's lesson in spoken form for their time capsule project.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>